From: OmacVM
Subject: [PATCH] virtio-input: a keyboard's start and reset reach the LED notifier (#784)

What: when a virtio keyboard starts (the guest's driver sets DRIVER_OK),
the UIs hear it through the LED notifier, its LEDs unchanged
(qemu_input_handler_set_leds_mask with the LEDs it has). When it stops (the
guest resets it: a restart inside the VM, QEMU still running), its LED state
goes back to none lit, and the UIs hear that.

Why: virtio-input drops events while the guest's driver is not up, and the
guest's driver reports only LED changes, starting from none lit. A Caps Lock
press OmacVM's Cocoa UI sent before the driver was up (the Mac's Caps Lock
on while the VM boots) went nowhere and nothing showed it; the start
report lets omacvm-cocoa-caps-lock.patch take the guest's lock from its LED
then. Kept across a reset, the old LEDs (Caps Lock on before the restart)
stayed lit for the UI for good, and the restarted guest's Caps Lock was
taken for on.

Only keyboards (the handler takes keys); a stop is reported only when an LED
was lit. Mice, tablets and the multitouch device are not touched. Other
UIs (VNC, spice, D-Bus) hear the same LEDs again at a start: no change.

Upstream: upstreamable (VNC, spice and D-Bus read the same stale mask).
Test: app/runtime/Tests/keys/test-caps-lock.sh (the model's start and reset;
with the patched sources: both are there); rows C5b/C6 of
work/run-1114/caps-796.
Applies after qemu-cocoa-precise-scroll.patch.

--- a/hw/input/virtio-input-hid.c
+++ b/hw/input/virtio-input-hid.c
@@ -226,7 +226,25 @@
 
     if (vinput->active) {
         qemu_input_handler_activate(vhid->hs);
+        /*
+         * OmacVM (#784): the UIs hear that a keyboard started (its LEDs as
+         * they are). Events before went nowhere: virtio_input_send drops them
+         * while the driver is not up, and the guest's driver reports only LED
+         * changes, so a press lost then would not show otherwise.
+         */
+        if (vhid->handler->mask & INPUT_EVENT_MASK_KEY) {
+            qemu_input_handler_set_leds_mask(vhid->hs, vhid->ledstate);
+        }
     } else {
+        /*
+         * OmacVM (#784): a reset device has no LED lit, and the guest's next
+         * driver reports only changes from there. Kept, the old LEDs (Caps
+         * Lock on before a restart inside the VM) stayed lit for the UI.
+         */
+        if ((vhid->handler->mask & INPUT_EVENT_MASK_KEY) && vhid->ledstate) {
+            vhid->ledstate = 0;
+            qemu_input_handler_set_leds_mask(vhid->hs, 0);
+        }
         qemu_input_handler_deactivate(vhid->hs);
     }
 }
